title: panel:open
tags: [[GUI command]] [[Panel slots and layout]]
kind: reference
summary: Open a panel type here, in the other slot, or in a new workspace

`panel:open <target> <type>` opens a panel type, at one of the three
[[open targets|Panel slots and layout]]:

* `here` — in place of the panel of the slot the command runs in; the header's panel menu does the same (<<key "panel:open here metarecord-list" "t l">> and the other `t` sequences);
* `other` — for the same workspace, in the other slot, opening it if it was hidden and taking it over if it showed another workspace — what the `↗` button of a `metarecord-list` row does ([[Cross-panel selection]]);
* `new` — in a new workspace, a copy of this one ([[workspace:fork]]), so the panel opens on the same state.

It is refused when the other visible slot already shows that type for the same
workspace. See [[Panel types]].

!! Opening a viewer of the selection

`file` and `metarecord-detail` show the workspace's selection, and opening one is
how a row is opened ([[Opening a row]]): <<key "panel:open other file" Enter>> and
<<key "panel:open other metarecord-detail" Shift+Enter>> in the lists. So `panel:open`
refuses a viewer with nothing of the selection to show, and says why on the status
bar — before moving anywhere (no slot switched, no workspace forked):

* `file`, when the selection is a metarecord without a file or folder (`selected_metarecord` set, `selected_paths` empty);
* `metarecord-detail`, when it is a path no metarecord records (`selected_paths` set, `selected_metarecord` null — an untracked entry of the file manager).

With no selection at all, both open as any panel type does: a layout can be set up
before anything is selected.
